<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Plan 53 §7 B7 / §5.4. ONE RECORD PRESS IS ONE UNDO STEP, AND A PASS THAT
  PLACES NOTHING IS NO STEP AT ALL.

  SApplication::stopRecording called the two recorders' stop()s in sequence and
  EACH opened its own macro, so a project with an armed audio track and an armed
  MIDI track cost TWO undos. sapplication.h documented "one undo step per
  recorder", which was both the intent and the bug: one press is one gesture.
  Worse, the count VARIED with what was captured, because the audio recorder
  returns before its macro when it has nothing placeable — so an empty audio
  half plus a MIDI half was one step and a full pass was two.

  WHY THE MACRO IS OPENED LAZILY, which is the part that is easy to get wrong.
  A Qt macro that closes with no commands in it is still a command ON the stack.
  So an outer beginMacro/endMacro around a pass that placed nothing would leave
  an undo entry that undoes nothing, and QUndoStack::isClean() would report the
  project dirty after a take that recorded nothing. SApplication therefore ARMS
  a scope and the FIRST submitAction inside it opens the macro; the recorders
  open none of their own, because nesting theirs inside would fix the count and
  re-create the empty-macro problem one level down.

  THE EMPTY PASS IS DETERMINISTIC, and getting there took two attempts. The
  file input backend ALWAYS captures something, so an armed AUDIO track can
  never give an empty pass. Plan 53 suggests a punch region behind the locator;
  that does empty the window, but SAudioRecorder's punch-out calls its own
  stop() on a TICK, so whether the take is still running when record-stop
  arrives is timing — measured both ways across two runs of this case. What
  IS deterministic: arm only the MIDI track and replay nothing. record-start
  refuses only when NEITHER recorder could start, the MIDI recorder captures no
  events, and its commit loop skips every empty pass, so it submits nothing.

  AND THE SAVE BEFORE IT IS LOAD-BEARING. assert-unsaved-changes reads
  QUndoStack::isClean(), which is only meaningful relative to a clean point —
  without the save, the earlier take would still be on the stack and the
  assertion would be about that, not about the empty pass.

  THE EMPTY PASS ALSO COVERS A SECOND COMMIT PATH, which writing this case is
  how I found. SAudioRecorder's PUNCH-OUT calls its own stop() DIRECTLY, never
  going through SApplication::stopRecording() — so a fix that wrapped only
  stopRecording would leave a punch-out's placements unwrapped, one undo step
  per segment per track. Both recorders therefore open the press scope around
  their own commit as well, and the scope is re-entrant so the two nest. The
  refused record-stop below is what pins that the take ended on that path.

  WHAT THIS CASE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ASSERT: that a SECOND undo finds no
  further step. undo does not distinguish step kinds, so it would pop the
  arm-track or set-track-input above and the assertion would be about those.
  "One undo removed BOTH halves" is the claim, and on main it fails because one
  undo removes only one of them.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="record_mixed_one_undo">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- Track 0 audio, track 1 MIDI. Monitoring OFF on both: this case is
         about what was RECORDED, not about what was heard. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="0" mode="off"/>
    <set-monitor-mode trackPath="1" mode="off"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="audio:qxa-record-input:1"/>
    <set-track-input trackPath="1" input="midi:capture:any"/>

    <set-locator position="0"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="1" armed="1"/>

    <!-- ONE PRESS, both halves. -->
    <record-start/>
    <midi-in-replay filePath="../midi_perf4.txt" startFrame="24000"/>
    <wait-ms ms="1200"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- Both halves landed. Floors, not equalities: how much a real-time pass
         captures is load-dependent, and the structural claim here is "each
         recorder placed something", not how much. -->
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" minClips="1"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="1" minClips="1"/>

    <!-- ...AND ONE UNDO TAKES BOTH AWAY. On main this leaves the MIDI half
         behind, because each recorder had its own macro. -->
    <undo count="1"/>
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="0"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="1" clips="0"/>

    <!-- —— the empty pass ——————————————————————— -->

    <!-- Set up the empty pass FIRST, then take the clean point. arm-track is
         an UNDOABLE action, so disarming after the save would dirty the stack
         and every assertion below would be about that rather than about the
         empty pass. Measured: it failed 3 runs out of 3 that way round. -->
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="0"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>

    <!-- Clean point, so isClean() below means something. -->
    <save-project path="../../build/test-output/record_mixed_one_undo/rmou.qxp"/>
    <assert-unsaved-changes expect="0"/>

    <!-- AN EMPTY PASS, DETERMINISTICALLY. Disarm the audio track and replay
         no MIDI: the MIDI recorder starts (which is all record-start needs,
         since it refuses only when NEITHER recorder could start), captures no
         events, and its commit loop skips every empty pass, so it submits
         nothing at all.

         THE PUNCH ROUTE PLAN 53 SUGGESTS DOES NOT WORK HERE, and the reason is
         worth writing down. A punch region behind the locator does empty the
         window, but SAudioRecorder's punch-out calls its own stop() on a TICK,
         so whether the take is still running when record-stop arrives is
         timing. Measured both ways on this box across two runs of this very
         case: once refused, once accepted. A gate cannot rest on that. -->
    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="400"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- NOTHING was pushed. An empty Qt macro would make this fail, which is
         exactly why the macro is opened lazily rather than around the stops. -->
    <assert-unsaved-changes expect="0"/>
    <assert-recorded-clip trackPath="0" clips="0"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="1" clips="0"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
